00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:01.130
Yes, I'm fine now.

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:03.530
I still think you ought to see a doctor.

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:05.420
No, I'm all right. See, I'm not even bleeding.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:09.170
Not on the outside, maybe, but what about on the inside?

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:13.120
I mean, I've heard of accidents where guys are hurt really bad and they don't even know it.

00:29:13.510 --> 00:29:14.330
Look, I'm fine.

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:20.100
Sure, all I'm trying to say is I'd hate to be responsible if you started a hemorrhage or something.

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:21.830
It wasn't my fault.

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:24.450
I mean, you admitted that yourself, but even so...

00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:27.270
Look, I signed a paper of solving you with any blame. Is that enough?

00:29:27.810 --> 00:29:29.880
Yeah. Don't get sore, Mr.

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:31.510
I'm just worried about you, is all.

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:33.570
Mr.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:41.910
It's none of my business, but how come you were wandering around out there in the middle of nowhere?

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:44.910
I don't know.

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:47.590
Thank you.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:49.070
I'm sorry to have caused you any trouble.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:51.060
Mr. I'm just glad you're alive.

00:31:12.060 --> 00:31:13.760
Darling, I've been calling every 15 minutes.

00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:14.660
I've been so worried.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:16.560
What happened?

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:19.420
I'm not sure.

00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:24.800
I don't know.

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:27.460
Colin.

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:29.980
Look, Jess, why don't you just...

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:38.060
forget about me. I don't know what's going on. Listen to me. I called Dr. Matthews and I made

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:48.400
an appointment for you for tomorrow morning. I'll be at the hotel to pick you up at 9.30. You'd do

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:58.860
that after everything that's happened? Yes, I would. Talbot, I didn't plan on falling in love with you.

00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:05.290
But I did, and I'm stuck with it, for better or for worse.

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:10.950
Well, thank you.

00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:13.440
Would you like me to come over now?

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:14.460
No.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:19.450
I'll see you tomorrow morning.

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:21.600
Good night, Jess.

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:27.240
Good night.

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:11.460
I've been waiting for you.

00:34:26.780 --> 00:34:27.440
You're real?

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:29.460
Well, that's a matter of opinion.

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:32.350
I used to think so years ago.

00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:33.570
Now I'm not so sure.

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:36.710
Sit down, Alan, over there.

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:38.270
I'll pour us a drink.

00:34:40.680 --> 00:34:41.440
Go on, sit down.

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:42.680
There's nothing to be afraid of.

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:02.150
By the way, you almost killed me with those scissors.

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:02.810
Did you know that?

00:35:05.280 --> 00:35:06.400
It's a shame you didn't.

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:10.460
Then nothing ever works out right for me.

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:16.100
See how close it was?

00:35:20.590 --> 00:35:21.370
Who are you?

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:22.830
You already know that.

00:35:22.830 --> 00:35:23.370
You must.

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:24.470
How else could you have found me?

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:26.110
I looked you up in the phone book.

00:35:26.450 --> 00:35:27.130
Yeah, but how...

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:32.140
I see. You've been to Coralville, haven't you?

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:33.820
Yes.

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:38.450
And you know what?

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:40.540
Your name.

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:41.430
That's all?

00:35:42.380 --> 00:35:43.160
That's all.

00:35:43.910 --> 00:35:45.830
Well, in that case, you better have some of that drink.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:04.570
Exactly eight days ago, you were born here in this house.

00:36:05.570 --> 00:36:05.970
What?

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:09.230
I made the delivery myself.

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:15.340
You're drunk.

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:16.660
A little bit.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:19.420
Not drunk enough.

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:22.060
Will you join me?

00:36:22.580 --> 00:36:23.880
I guarantee no hangover.

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:24.880
Wasn't that thoughtful of me?

00:36:26.200 --> 00:36:27.900
Look, I'm in no mood for jokes, mister.

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:29.980
Well, that's a pity, but...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.320
because this whole thing is a joke.

00:36:31.700 --> 00:36:32.740
Well, I'm not laughing.

00:36:33.860 --> 00:36:36.700
You will, because the joke's on me.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:38.140
Would you get to the point?

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:40.490
Well, you've been to Courville.

00:36:40.670 --> 00:36:42.450
You know that Alan Talbot never lived there.

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:45.710
You know you've been behaving oddly of late,

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:49.310
judging from that handkerchief on your arm.

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:51.730
I'd say you know about that, too.

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.430
So, with all this information,

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:57.090
what do I need to tell you?

00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:01.280
Who am I?

00:37:03.280 --> 00:37:04.360
You're nobody, Alan.

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:06.380
Nobody at all.

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:08.180
Stop it, Walter.

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:10.830
Well, who is this watch I'm wearing?

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:11.570
Ask me that.

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:14.500
Who is the refrigerator in the kitchen?

00:37:15.350 --> 00:37:16.150
Don't you understand?

00:37:17.730 --> 00:37:18.110
No.

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:22.070
You're a machine, Alan.

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:23.370
A mechanical device.

00:37:25.300 --> 00:37:26.280
I don't believe it.

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:27.820
Blame you.

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:28.660
I wouldn't believe it either.

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:31.000
It's true.

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:36.500
The fact is you were born a long time ago inside my head.

00:37:37.180 --> 00:37:38.720
All kids have dreams, don't they?

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:40.210
You were mine.

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:44.550
The others thought about joining the army or flying to Mars.

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:47.100
And they finally grew up and they forgot their dreams.

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:48.920
I didn't.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:53.380
I thought about one thing and longed for one thing, always.

00:37:55.600 --> 00:37:56.180
Just one.

00:37:58.580 --> 00:37:59.960
Perfect artificial magic.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:12.720
not a robot a duplicate of a human being it was harmless not even very imaginative for a child

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:22.610
then I became an adult only somewhere along the line I forgot to grow up like most geniuses

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:29.810
kept my dream I create

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:38.950
you, Alan. Is that straight enough for you? You're not only drunk, you're crazy. How would

00:38:38.950 --> 00:38:46.810
you explain that? Well, it's an artificial arm. I don't know. You expect me to believe

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:52.920
I'm a machine when I know I'm not? That's ridiculous. I eat, I drink, I sleep. Didn't

00:38:52.920 --> 00:38:59.210
I tell you I wanted a perfect creation, Alan?

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:06.140
I want you to come with me.

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:08.520
Where?

00:39:10.070 --> 00:39:12.680
Well, let's call it the delivery room.

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:15.840
You want to see where you were born?

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:39.800
birthplace.

00:40:56.240 --> 00:40:56.520
of this

00:41:00.760 --> 00:41:05.660
Alan Talbot the first, Alan Talbot the second.

00:41:08.690 --> 00:41:09.850
Factory rejects.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:11.980
Any questions?

00:41:14.360 --> 00:41:16.020
Where did you get the money for all this?

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:20.120
I told you, I'm a genius.

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:23.320
When I found out how much I'd need for my work,

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:25.420
I developed an improved calculator.

00:41:25.840 --> 00:41:28.240
Try electronics, turns out $3,000 or $4,000 a year.

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:29.160
I get the royalties.

00:41:30.190 --> 00:41:31.470
The money was the least of it.

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:34.960
Failures, though, they were bad.

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:38.950
I can't tell you how many times I failed and had to start over again.

00:41:40.240 --> 00:41:41.760
And you did this all by yourself?

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:44.480
No, that wouldn't have been possible.

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:47.900
I'm afraid the day of the lone inventor has passed.

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:51.500
I had some of the best scientific minds in the world assisting me.

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:54.220
Of course, they didn't take the project seriously.

00:41:54.460 --> 00:41:55.500
To them, it was just a game.

00:41:55.780 --> 00:41:57.560
A lot of theoretical war gathering.

00:41:58.500 --> 00:41:59.980
How would it be possible to create a...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:05.560
entire nervous system that worked like a real one how could the brain be duplicated with all its

00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:13.820
subtleties you see i was after real intelligence mere reactions weren't enough those mannequins

00:42:13.820 --> 00:42:21.620
can react my creation had to have a memory he had to have abstract reasoning power

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:29.700
past a personality millions of intricate facets multiplied by millions to make true intelligence

00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:37.070
Now, to accomplish this from the beginning would have been possible.

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:40.520
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:42:40.580 --> 00:42:42.480
What do you mean you decided to use yourself?

00:42:42.980 --> 00:42:43.580
Just that.

00:42:44.770 --> 00:42:46.850
In certain of your cells, I made certain impressions.

00:42:47.500 --> 00:42:49.320
My own memories went into the cells.

00:42:49.940 --> 00:42:51.920
Some of my talents, some of my knowledge.

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:53.820
Bits and pieces of myself.

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:56.660
So now you're telling me that I'm you?

00:42:58.960 --> 00:42:59.740
Oh, that's impossible.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:10.780
on me this is my flesh non-conductive plastic i admit it feels like flesh but then that was the

00:43:10.780 --> 00:43:18.810
idea you see i cushioned the mechanical parts with a foam layer and then i worked out of that's not

00:43:18.810 --> 00:43:27.770
what you want to hear is it you lived in courville yes for your past i gave you my memories of the

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:29.760
town some of them must have been

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:34.070
accurate and incomplete. I left Coorville 20 years ago, but going there must have been quite an

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:45.270
experience. It was. What about the university? Fictitious. I had to give you a job. At Mildred.

00:43:45.660 --> 00:43:51.220
All the old ladies I've ever known rolled into one. After my parents died, I lived alone.

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:58.720
The reputation as a Don Juan is, I regret to say, imaginary.

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:04.560
That's about it, Alan. The rest you can fill in, up to last week, anyway.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:05.920
What about last week?

00:44:07.280 --> 00:44:08.640
I wish I knew. Something went wrong.

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:13.780
I couldn't understand it. You suddenly attacked me with a pair of scissors and then ran away.

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:16.880
As you know, I've been unable to find your sense.

00:44:18.090 --> 00:44:18.810
What's wrong with me?

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:22.480
I'm not sure. Whatever it is, that's wrong with me, too.

00:44:24.110 --> 00:44:26.510
We're all potential murderers, Alan, all of us.

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:29.970
But if we're normal, we're protected by our inhibition.

00:44:30.030 --> 00:44:32.390
You've wanted to kill?

00:44:32.570 --> 00:44:34.030
Of course, everybody has.

00:44:35.230 --> 00:44:38.330
But my own inhibitions have held my impulses in check.

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:41.500
I couldn't kill, no matter how much I might want to.

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:43.220
So what do you say?

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:45.140
With you, something went wrong.

00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:48.650
To put it even clearer, Alan, you're insane.

00:44:57.130 --> 00:44:57.490
Treated?

00:44:58.000 --> 00:44:58.780
Repaired, you mean?

00:44:59.820 --> 00:44:59.960
I...

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:05.960
don't know why not well as much as i hate to admit it luck had a great deal to do with your

00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:13.980
creation skill alone wasn't enough you saying i'm an accident i'm afraid so you see alan i was like

00:45:13.980 --> 00:45:19.460
a blind man with a machine gun i kept firing and reloading and firing and finally i hit the target

00:45:19.460 --> 00:45:23.970
but it was off center i don't even know if i could come close again that's the story of my life

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:29.610
right down the line success in the little things failure in the big ones

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.240
I told myself I wanted to make an artificial man,

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:38.820
but I think what I really wanted was to build another Walter Ryder,

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:44.710
only without the drawbacks, sort of reverse Jekyll and Hyde.

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:47.930
Everything I wished I was, my creation would be.

00:45:49.070 --> 00:45:51.280
Where I was shy, he would be aggressive.

00:45:52.030 --> 00:45:55.230
Where I was cold and withdrawn, he would be warm and outgoing.

00:45:55.870 --> 00:45:57.510
Loneliness would be unknown to him.

00:45:58.240 --> 00:45:59.900
Disease and pain and death.

00:46:00.730 --> 00:46:01.430
All unknown.

00:46:02.080 --> 00:46:03.300
He would be unselfish.

00:46:04.960 --> 00:46:07.000
He would be ambitious, but not fanatical.

00:46:07.830 --> 00:46:10.100
And, oh, Alan, he would be perfect.

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:13.790
That was my dream, a perfect version of myself.

00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:23.010
That's right. You should.

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:27.820
There's somebody else, Walter. A girl.

00:46:28.250 --> 00:46:29.710
We were going to be married next week.

00:46:31.050 --> 00:46:31.610
Is she pretty?

00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:35.270
Pretty, intelligent, and lonely.

00:46:36.030 --> 00:46:37.530
Something you ought to be able to understand.

00:46:41.240 --> 00:46:41.900
Sorry, Alan.

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:43.990
Well, that's a great consolation.

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:47.560
You sure you can't fix me?

00:46:48.900 --> 00:46:50.800
Even if I could, it wouldn't solve your problems.

00:46:50.800 --> 00:46:52.100
The girl would find out eventually.

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:52.990
How?

00:46:53.880 --> 00:46:56.760
Well, for one thing, she would grow old, and you wouldn't.

00:46:57.360 --> 00:46:59.700
For another, being a machine, you would suffer a breakdown.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.270
day and all of these things never occurred to you i guess i didn't really think i'd succeed as well

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:15.120
as i did what does it matter anyway there's nothing that can be done yes there is you're

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:22.540
gonna create another alan talbot no i just told you you heard me another alan talbot only this

00:47:22.980 --> 00:47:27.380
one's gonna be right and he's gonna walk out of here tonight and he's gonna marry a girl named

00:47:27.380 --> 00:47:29.720
jess and for the first time in his

00:47:29.720 --> 00:47:29.980
mi

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:42.270
life he's gonna be happy I don't understand you give me a pencil this is

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:49.860
her name and address she said she would meet me at 9 30 in the morning but

00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:51.600
she'll be home now

00:48:08.290 --> 00:48:09.490
What's the matter, Alan? What is it?

00:48:17.320 --> 00:48:17.900
Then you take it.

00:48:18.490 --> 00:48:19.250
Take it and get out.

00:48:20.060 --> 00:48:20.360
Now.

00:48:21.380 --> 00:48:21.900
Now hurry!

00:49:14.840 --> 00:49:15.380
Who is it?

00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:21.440
Junior Woodchuck's.

00:49:30.250 --> 00:49:30.770
Come in, Alan.

00:49:32.740 --> 00:49:43.840
Why are you looking at me like that?

00:49:46.670 --> 00:49:47.330
I don't know.

00:49:52.420 --> 00:49:53.080
So worried.

00:49:54.920 --> 00:49:56.440
Yesterday, I didn't...

00:49:56.470 --> 00:49:57.490
Forget yesterday.

00:49:59.100 --> 00:49:59.980
Whatever happened...

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:00.480
Forget that.

00:50:02.820 --> 00:50:03.580
But, Alan...

00:50:03.580 --> 00:50:04.000
Please.

00:50:05.230 --> 00:50:07.170
We just had a rotten nightmare, that's all.

00:50:07.570 --> 00:50:09.670
Now we're awake and everything's going to be fine.

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:12.960
Will you try to believe that?

00:50:15.260 --> 00:50:15.740
Do you?

00:50:17.500 --> 00:50:17.760
Yes.

00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:26.560
And so do I.

00:50:29.020 --> 00:50:29.760
If you...

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:31.500
I promise to tell me about it someday.

00:50:32.600 --> 00:50:34.180
Promise? Someday?

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:37.030
Now, will you fix me a cup of coffee?

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:38.760
Sure.

00:50:40.120 --> 00:50:41.620
Would you like something to eat?

00:50:44.200 --> 00:50:47.300
How about a couple of Mother Connelly's hand-fried eggs?

00:50:47.880 --> 00:50:49.940
They're guaranteed to make a new man out of it.

00:50:55.320 --> 00:50:55.600
Thank you.

00:51:12.600 --> 00:51:15.900
It can be said that Walter Ryder succeeded in his life's ambition,

00:51:16.830 --> 00:51:19.150
even though the man he created was, after all, himself.

00:51:20.330 --> 00:51:22.930
There may be easier ways to self-improvement,

00:51:22.990 --> 00:51:26.550
but sometimes it happens that the shortest distance between two points

00:51:26.550 --> 00:51:29.970
is a crooked line through the twilight zone.

00:51:36.460 --> 00:51:39.680
The ingredients, an American destroyer, the Pacific Ocean,

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:43.300
and the ghostly sound of hammering from 30 fathoms below,

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:47.120
they add up to a strange tale of the bizarre and nightmarish.

00:51:47.780 --> 00:51:51.420
Mike Kellen and Simon Oakland star in a very different kind of twilight zone,

00:51:51.720 --> 00:51:54.220
which we call the 30-fathom grave.

00:52:04.030 --> 00:52:07.150
They had a kick in the head. What do you suppose it is?

00:52:07.790 --> 00:52:08.950
Ghosts, man, ghosts.

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:12.700
Here it is. 714.

00:52:14.240 --> 00:52:16.690
Commissioned December 1941.

00:52:17.920 --> 00:52:19.260
Sunk in action.

00:52:20.920 --> 00:52:22.180
First Battle of the Solomons.

00:52:23.040 --> 00:52:25.970
August 7th, 1942.

00:52:27.640 --> 00:52:29.580
Well, that was 20 years ago.

00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:32.240
Well, Captain Beecham, who's down there?

00:52:33.180 --> 00:52:34.360
Who's inside that sub?

00:53:00.830 --> 00:53:01.210
Thank you.
